(Sihanoukville): A new medical laboratory building for the Kovid-19 test machine in Sihanoukville Provincial Referral Hospital will be completed by the end of March and is scheduled to be officially operational by the end of March. April 2021.
Sihanoukville provincial health officials say that after the completion of construction, a team of Chinese experts will facilitate the installation of Kovid-19 virus test equipment and train relevant health workers during early April 2021.
The Kovid-19 Virus Testing Equipment is a grant from China, while the building was donated by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross.
It should be informed that through the establishment of this medical laboratory, it will be easier for Sihanoukville and neighboring provinces to test for Kovid-19 without having to come to Phnom Penh as before.
KOICA Provides $ 2 Million to Develop Master Plan for Integrated Water Resources Management in the East Mekong Delta
(Korea): The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) has provided about $ 2 million to develop a master plan for integrated water resources management in the delta east of the Mekong River. The fundraising ceremony was held on March 25, 2021.
The financing agreement was signed by Rho Hyunjun, Director of KOICA Cambodia, and Mr. Ponh Sachchak, Secretary of State at the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology.
The project aims to reduce the impact of natural disasters such as floods in the rainy season and drought in the dry season, and to increase agricultural production for the betterment of economic development in the country as well as in Local. In addition, the project aims to improve the quality of life of people in the target area by increasing farmers' incomes.
Under a grant of approximately $ 2 million from the Republic of Korea, the project will be implemented in 24 months (from 2021 to 2023) in Prey Veng and Svay Rieng provinces. Key components of the project include: development of a master plan for integrated water resources management for the Mekong Delta, feasibility study and capacity development support.
The project will develop a long-term development plan and conduct preliminary feasibility studies as a recommendation and additional input for the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology. Human resources at both policy and technical levels will be provided as local and international training. In addition, Korean experts will be sent to work with Cambodian experts to implement the project, as well as to share experiences with the Cambodian team.
Mr. Ponh Sachchak said that this project is a necessary need for Cambodia because the master plan is an important input for the agricultural water sector in Cambodia, especially in the delta of the Mekong River. It is a roadmap for Cambodia to manage water efficiently and effectively, with water resources being allocated not only for agriculture, but also for people's livelihoods and beyond.
He added that the Mekong region is at the heart of the South Korean policy of seeking strong cooperation with Southeast Asian countries through four pillars: people, prosperity, peace and the planet.
