(Phnom Penh): Cambodia is a country rich in biodiversity, as well as many endangered or rare species of plants and wildlife. Cambodia's biodiversity index was scored at 7.5 in 1997, but so far the score has been higher, depending on later research.
Therefore, to better understand that there are currently endangered species as well as a few rare species living in the protected areas of natural resources, please find out through this article:
In Cambodia, many endangered and rare species are listed on the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List, including 39 mammals, 36 species of birds and reptiles. Many more.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment noted that in the last five years, the presence of wildlife has increased, including some rare and endangered species, due to the efforts of the authorities to conserve and protect wildlife habitats. The presence of these wildlife is full of hope for the Cambodian people and for the benefit of the world's biodiversity, as some of the world's most endangered species are still present in the forests of Cambodia.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that according to the 4th National Biodiversity Report, Cambodia is a biodiversity-rich country with 123 species of mammals, 545 species of birds, 88 species of animals, 874 species of fish, 2,308 species of plants. 24 species of hard corals, 14 types of soft corals, 10 types of seagrass and 63 species of sea urchins.
However, there are still some species of plants and animals that have not been studied and identified. There are 39 species of plants and animals on the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List: 36 species of mammals, 36 species of birds, 13 species of mammals, 38 species of small plants and 12 species of mammals.
The wildlife and plants on the planet are invaluable in terms of ecosystems, society, economics, science, education, culture and human development, "he said. Also sustainable, while some of the world's endangered wildlife and birds are present in Cambodia.
Cambodia, as a member of the United Nations, is ready to cooperate internationally with all countries in conserving natural resources and preventing and combating illegal wildlife trafficking. Cambodia has been actively involved in the fight against illegal wildlife trafficking, and Cambodia is also cooperating as an international partner with the United Kingdom in preventing and suppressing illegal wildlife trafficking in the world.
