Cambodia is training dogs to sniff for Kovid-19 carriers
(Phnom Penh): A group of dog technicians from CMAC in collaboration with the Ministry of Health has been discussing, researching and developing dog sniffing techniques for coronavirus carriers (Kovid 19). This study was conducted under the guidance of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
After receiving the recommendation from Samdech Techo Hun Sen, on March 24, 2021, Mr. Heng Ratana, the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC), inspected the work of dogs at the Technical Institute of Activities. Min Simak, Kampong Chhnang Province.
Inspection of Mr. Heng Ratana's work on dogs in preparation for research and development of dog sniffing techniques for coronavirus carriers (Kovid 19).
CMAC prepares dogs, prepares training documents, organizes training facilities and specialists for this research, in collaboration with medical scientists of the Ministry of Health.
Please note that some countries such as Germany, Finland, Australia, Russia, etc. have been using dogs to help sniff out the carrier virus.
In the past, Cambodia used to buy dogs from other countries for tens of thousands of dollars, but now Cambodia can breed its own dogs and export more.
Currently, CMAC has dogs capable of supporting counter-terrorism, mine and UXO clearance, and environmental work.
