BANGLADESH: A humanitarian team on Wednesday is working to reunite Rohingya Muslim families after the world's largest refugee camp in Bangladesh caught fire. This was released by Reuters on Wednesday evening, March 24, 2021.
The blaze engulfed the camp, which was home to a large number of refugees, earlier Monday. According to the United Nations, the incident left 15 people dead and hundreds missing, while Bangladesh claimed at least 11 dead.
Humanitarian groups in Bangladesh say that during the fire, most of the refugees were evacuated to other camps to stay with friends or relatives temporarily. But many children were separated from their families during the evacuation as humanitarian teams tried to reunite the families of the refugees.
There are about 1 million Rohingya refugees living in large camps in Cox's Bazar, southeastern Bangladesh. The Muslims have little hope of returning to their homeland in Myanmar, where most of them have been denied citizenship and are being persecuted.
Three Cambodian Organizations Receive $ 60,000 from the Art of Sharing
(Phnom Penh): Three organizations in Cambodia, including the Cambodian Organization for the Elderly, the Cambodian Intercultural Psychosocial Organization (TPO Cambodia) and the Cambodian Social Development Organization, received $ 60,000 each. $ 20,000 was raised from an exhibition on the "Art of Sharing" held at the Isanvarman Museum of Economics and Currency.
This is the first phase of handing over the charitable funds raised for use in 2021 and will continue to receive the remaining funds in 2022 and 2023 in accordance with the actual activities of each organization.
According to the organizers, such a phase-by-phase disbursement helps maintain a sustainable budget for the organization and benefits the community in the long run.
Please be informed that 3 months ago, Ms. Chea Serey and Ms. Adana Mam-Le Gros, the owner of the artwork, initiated this charity exhibition "The Art of Sharing" to share a positive message about the continued hope and solidarity in This difficult and stressful time. A total of more than 20 paintings by the two artists have been on public display for nearly two months at the U.S. Museum and on social media.
So far, all the paintings on display have been sold or auctioned off, which is a large part of the money donated to the three beneficiaries today.
According to the organizers of the charity exhibition "The Art of Sharing", it was a resounding success and exceeded all expectations.
Ms. Chea Serey said, "I am very pleased to see the success of the art exhibition of sharing and overwhelming support from all of you. "We started this exhibition for the benefit of society and to inspire a sense of solidarity. I can say that we have achieved all the goals of fundraising and sharing the message of this program."
"I would also like to thank all the uncles and aunts who visited the exhibition in person or on social media, helped buy paintings or souvenirs, donated money and showed support for the event," she said.
"I am thrilled to see the support and love of so many sharing art exhibitions," said Adana Mam-Legros. It is a testament to the fact that we can make a lot of positive changes when we are united in good faith, no matter how difficult the circumstances. "Funds raised from the sale of paintings will be used to support the activities of the three beneficiaries as planned."
"We are honored and pleased to be selected as one of the beneficiaries of the Shared Art Exhibition," said Chhay Voodin, Executive Director of the Cambodian Society for the Advancement of Cambodian Society. The charitable funds raised through this exhibition will make a significant contribution to supporting the operations and activities of our organization. ”
"Today has been a great day for us, but there are still many local organizations working on important projects that need help and support," he added. Therefore, I hope that there will be more philanthropists organizing such activities in the future.
