KOICA provides $ 7 million to the Ministry of Education to increase information technology education capacity
(Phnom Penh): The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) has provided US $ 7 million to support the capacity of information technology education at the Provincial Regional Teacher Training Center and the Lower Secondary School in Cambodia.
According to a press release from KOICA, the project focuses on the contribution of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to improve the qualifications of trainers and teachers in information technology education at provincial pedagogical centers and primary schools in Kampong Cham, Prey Veng, Takeo and Kandal provinces.
Through this project, KOICA will provide master scholarships to trainers, update curricula, textbooks, computer equipments, and improve computer labs at regional and provincial pedagogical centers and upper secondary schools.
At the signing ceremony, Mr. Rho Hyunjun emphasized that "KOICA hopes that this project will improve information technology education in Cambodia as well as contribute to supporting the strategic goals of the Royal Government of Cambodia in human resource development. Period of strengthening the quality of education, science and technology.
The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) was established in 1991 under the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Republic of Korea, a government agency that provides Korea grant programs.
KOICA aims to promote friendly relations, cooperation and exchanges with developing countries through the Official Development Assistance (ODA) program for their socio-economic development.
