Which countries impose sanctions against the Myanmar military regime? (Video inside) (Washington): The West, led by the United States, has strongly condemned the military coup in Myanmar, urging the generals to stop using violence against protesters and release those trapped. The communes include Aung San Suu Kyi to be free and return power to Suu Kyi's elected civilian government.
At the same time, the United States condemned the military regime in Myanmar, and some Western countries began to follow in Washington's footsteps, sanctioning generals, including those involved in the February 1 coup. And cracking down on protesters to force the Myanmar military to change its mind.
But so far, the Myanmar military does not seem to be ignoring the condemnation or appeal, and instead the situation in Myanmar is getting worse. According to civil society organizations in Myanmar, at least 261 protesters have been shot dead by security forces since February 1.
The following are the sanctions against Myanmar:
US President Joe Biden issued an operation order on February 1, paving the way for sanctions against the Myanmar military. The order freezes the $ 1 billion in assets held by the US Federal Reserve in New York, which the Myanmar military has sought to seize after the coup.
As of March 22, the US Treasury Department has announced the latest sanctions, including the inclusion of a police chief and a commander of the Myanmar military regime, along with two military units, on the US sanctions blacklist. They are involved in cracking down on protesters.
Australia on March 7 also suspended some cooperation with the Myanmar military and said it would divert government aid to civil society groups.
Some analysts believe that sanctions may not change the military, and that it will not improve the situation in Myanmar. Because sanctions usually hurt civilians more severely than their leaders. But for the United States, sanctions are a protection of democratic values and human rights.
This confirmation was made through an announcement made on March 22, 2021. The declaration states that individuals who are obliged to wear a mask include:
* Positive Kovid-19 during face-to-face meetings with health officials or other competent officials
* A person who is obliged to perform the ritual, except that person performs the ritual in a separate room alone and not in person
* Drivers of all types of vehicles that are public transportation or transportation for the public.
* Passengers by public transportation or public transportation
Direct customer service providers include front-line staff in enterprises or establishments or business locations, officers and health workers, physiotherapist providers, individuals working in restaurants or canteens, inn or hotel staff, Employees who provide massage services and other individuals who provide direct services with clients
The declaration also states the geographical location of the masked duty. The practice of wearing masks is carried out in geographical areas where there is an outbreak of 19 Kovid in the community, which is determined by the notification of the Ministry of Health.
For geographically non-communicable diseases in the community, the Ministry encourages individuals to adhere to the "3 preventive and 3 preventive" measures to prevent the transmission or transmission of 19 cases of HIV.
The statement said that those who do not perform their duties of wearing a mask or keeping their safety gaps shall be punished as provided in the Sub-Decree on Health Rules to Prevent the Spread of Kovid 19 and its Vicious and Dangerous Infectious Diseases. Other serious. Article 19 of the sub-decree states that those who do not wear a mask shall be punished by a transitional fine from 200,000 riels to 1,000,000 riels.
However, the statement also stated that individuals were exempted from wearing masks. Individuals exempt from wearing a mask include:
* Individuals who can not wear a mask for physical or health reasons with a doctor's confirmation
* Individuals living in a private residence together during a gathering in that residence
* During private travel, close alone or with a person living in the same residence
* For athletes during training or playing sports in official sporting events where wearing a mask is not possible
* In circumstances where it is necessary to remove the mask to identify the individual or to receive medical or other necessary services
* In the event of an activity that requires the removal of a mask, for example, when eating and drinking or when taking photographs for identification in official documents.
The obligation to wear a mask may also be waived for artists performing in art shows authorized by the competent authorities or individuals to speak at formal events or meetings.
Ministry of Cults and Religions Issues Additional Guidelines for Preventing 19 Infectious Diseases During a 3-Day Holiday on National New Year
The Ministry of Cults and Religions would like to introduce and remind the leaders, civil servants and staff of the Ministry of Cults and Religions, as well as the leaders of religions and units under all ministries to adopt a clean life by changing habits and attitudes. Extremely vigilant and continue to prevent the transmission of Kovid-19, anytime and anywhere, and comply with applicable laws and regulations.
The Ministry would like to make the following additional instructions:
1. Monasteries and other churches must ensure respect for and implement health measures to prevent and prevent the spread of Kovid-19 disease and to maintain a safe distance from one person to another at least one and a half meters when standing. Sit, stand, wait, chat or in other places.
2: Implement the "3 prevent and 3 do not" measures as well as other necessary health measures consistently and everyone.
3: Avoid organizing religious ceremonies or gatherings that are noisy and crowded.
4. Increase vigilance in the current situation by regularly following the guidelines of the Ministry of Health and the World Health Organization (WHO).
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sim Sitha, Director General of Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority, said that Phnom Penh and Takhmao City, Kandal Province will close the shortage of clean water for full use between 2023-2025 when three water treatment plants. Recently opened between 2023-2025.
He stated that the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority has clearly considered and planned for the water use of the people in Phnom Penh and Takhmao City, Kandal Province, and the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority has a long-term master plan in cooperation with capital development partners. Invest in factory construction as well.
He added that for the investment in the construction of the factory recently opened the construction site of the water treatment plant in Bak Kheng, and the project has two stages, one completed in mid-2022 and one in mid-2023. Another step.
The two-stage Bak Kheng water supply investment project will use up to 381.4 million US dollars and the production capacity will reach 390 thousand cubic meters / day by the end of 2023, which is a record high capacity.
After the completion of the construction project of Bak Kheng Water Treatment Plant, Phase 1 and 2, Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority can supply clean water to an additional 1,040,000 people and has a total production capacity of 1 million cubic meters / day.
He said that in addition to the construction of a water treatment plant in Bak Kheng, the Water Supply Authority also plans to install water distribution pipes to the outskirts of the city more than 1,500 kilometers from this year to 2025.
Mr. Sim Sitha stated that in addition to the construction of a water treatment plant in Bakheng, the Water Supply Authority is also in the process of constructing two more water treatment plants, one in Takhmao City, Kandal Province, with a project of $ 30 million under a Japanese government grant. And another in Boeung Thom, near the 7 Makara Dam, west of Phnom Penh, with funding from the Czech government. The two new plants will start construction in 2021 and be completed in 2024.
He added that between 2023 and 2025, the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority can solve the problem of water shortage for the people and also implement the policy of the Royal Government to use 100% clean water in 2025.
The Director General said that the water shortage in the past, the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority has been resolved, however, see that in the dry season also face a shortage of clean water for use, so people Especially those in the city to save water so that people in the suburbs can get water for use.
He reminded that in case of water shortage in the dry season, the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority has also prepared for the people to have water pipes in 16 suburbs and water pipes can be used to extinguish fires. This provides water to people in other needy areas and distributes this water free of charge.
Meanwhile, the ministry also received another PCR machine, a grant from a Chinese friend, arrived last week to be installed in Sihanoukville after a team of experts from the People's Republic of China arrived in Phnom Penh. March 29 to come and help install this machine. Therefore, when there is a big machine for testing samples in Sihanoukville, all samples in Kep and Kampot will be able to transport samples for inspection in Sihanoukville.
