The territory of China at that time was not yet unified. It was not until 280 AD that Emperor Sumayan invaded the eastern kingdom of Wu in an attempt to reunite all the territories that had been divided since Christ. 220 AD then.
The victory over the Uyghur Kingdom marked the end of the history of the Three Kingdoms period, and the whole of ancient China was reunited under the auspices of the mighty Emperor Wu Jing.
Emperor Sumayan died on March 16, 290 CE, at the palace of Lakyang at the age of 54, and his son succeeded to Lokayang. Emperor Wu (Hui).
The kingdom of Jing above changed its capital three times. During the reign of Emperor Sumayan, he established the capital in Lokyang, and Lokyang remained the capital of Maha Jin until 311 AD.
