The 27-nation bloc plans to blacklist four Chinese officials and a state entity over Beijing's crackdown on the Uighurs after the bloc's ambassadors gave the green light last week. Last week.
The European Union will also freeze assets and visa bans on 11 Burmese military officials for last month's military coup and crackdown on protesters.
The symbolic move was reported by EU diplomats and is expected to be officially unveiled in its official newsletter on Monday. It is the first time Brussels has attacked Beijing over human rights abuses since the bloc imposed an arms embargo in 2015 after Anmen Square.
