Due to the efforts to better manage and conserve protected areas, the forest resources in protected areas are well protected and conserved, despite some challenges and minor offenses. Wildlife is seen beginning to increase in some rare species and some in constant numbers.
People are becoming more aware of the importance of protecting forest ecosystems. People living in protected areas receive additional income from the removal of non-timber forest products and the benefits of forest conservation.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Environment, has paid close attention to the protection and conservation of natural resources by preserving timber to sell the role of forests, which play an important role in protecting the planet's ecosystem through the sale of carbon credits in the carbon market. Voluntarily, the proceeds from the sale of carbon credits are used to support conservation work and economic development for local communities.
Cambodia is optimistic that Cambodia will be able to sell more carbon credits in the coming years, as from 2016 to the present, Cambodia has successfully sold carbon credits in the voluntary market with a budget of $ 11.6 million. This budget is used to protect and conserve forests and develop community protected areas.
So far, only Cambodia and Indonesia in ASEAN have sold carbon credits in the voluntary carbon market.
