One raccoon and two rhinos found dead in Preah Vihear wildlife sanctuary
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment and the Cambodian Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS) are deeply saddened by the loss of a raccoon and two giant trout that were killed by criminals this week.
These are two horrific incidents that happened at the same time in the Kulen-Prohm Tep Wildlife Sanctuary and Chep Wildlife Sanctuary in Preah Vihear Province.
The carcass of the raccoon was found by the community during a forest patrol on March 17, 2021 in Sambo village, Srayong commune, Kulen district, in the area of Kulen-Prohm Tep Wildlife Sanctuary. All that remains are bones, skin, and feet, and are likely to be burned to destroy the evidence.
The bodies of the two giant trout were found dead by the community in a tree with their legs tied to a trap in Dong Phlit village, Chhep Pei commune, Chhep district. The two giant reptiles were apparently trapped at a nearby lake and tried to break free from the snare and get caught in the air.
Although in the past it was abundant in Southeast Asia, it is now only present in Cambodia and is thought to be less than 300.
The raccoon is listed on the IUCN Red List as the world's most endangered species. The number of raccoons has dropped dramatically in the last few decades due to habitat loss and poaching.
According to IUCN data, the number of leopards is estimated at around 4,000 in the world. Currently, there are probably less than 1,500 raccoons across Cambodia.
The Ministry of Environment and WCS call on the general public to participate in protecting and conserving these rare wildlife species for the benefit of future generations. In particular, ask all criminals to stop hunting and trapping this wild animal immediately because it is a crime.
Taking this opportunity, the Ministry requested local authorities at all levels and stakeholders to continue to educate, disseminate and strengthen the implementation of the law to better protect and conserve forests and wildlife, especially rare species to survive. Senior Minister Sun Chanthol: Outstanding students at Techo Sen Institute of Public Works and Transport will be able to practice abroad
