General knowledge: Ancient and modern maritime demarcation
(World): The demarcation of the sea is very important for the countries bordering the sea to respect each other and avoid any abusive actions. In modern times, maritime demarcation is in line with the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), but in ancient times demarcation was not easy. What were the ancient and modern maritime boundaries like?
Therefore, we present only a few clear demarcations of the sea, such as the Treaty of Torresila, how to demarcate the sea through artillery fire, the 1930 International Law Establishment Conference, and the Declaration. Harry Truman, 1945
In 1951, 80% of World Trade Vessels were registered in countries with a 3-mile limit and 10% in 4-mile countries. Once the international community recognizes that coastal states have access to three or four nautical miles of territorial sea, then maritime issues are no longer an issue.
Discussions related to maritime law include the decision to define the territorial sea within 3 miles, the demands of some states on the expansion of the territorial sea, the acceptance of the applicable principles of jurisdiction over customs, health and security in the proposed link areas. Provide large-scale exclusive fishing grounds and common heritage concepts. Discussions at the conference did not address all of the issues, but identified and identified some.
In the first declaration, the United States claimed more than three miles of mineral resources in the highlands and submarine plateau. The statement also affirmed US jurisdiction over the resources of the seabed and submarine plateau. In the second declaration, the United States imposed regulations on fishing in the High Sea, which extends from its shores, but did not regulate navigation in that area.
The United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea defines the sea as follows:
In addition, the coastal states may exercise jurisdiction, both civil and criminal, on any foreign vessel navigating this internal waters.
As a result, coastal states have full rights to do business on the seabed and under the sea. Navigation of foreign ships in the territorial sea is limited, and islands above the territorial sea must also have permission from the coastal state.
The connecting area is the sea area, which measures 24 miles from the baseline to the open sea. In this interconnected area, coastal states can prevent violations of their laws, regulations, customs, immigration or health. On the other hand, coastal states can also take action against violations of laws and regulations that occur in the area.
In this junction, other states have the right to navigate, the right to fly over, but not the right to fish, to mine. As for the right to scientific research, cable laying, other states must have the consent of the coastal states.
In the exclusive economic zone, all states, whether coastal or non-coastal, are free to navigate, fly from above, lay cables, lay pipelines, and other uses in accordance with International law deals with these freedoms.
International High Seas The International Seas is a sea area that does not belong to any state. All countries around the world are free to use international maritime space, such as navigation, overhead pipeline, cable laying, fishing, scientific research and so on.
All states must adhere to the principle of peacekeeping in the use of international maritime space, and the use of military force is prohibited.
In the highlands, coastal states have the right to exercise their sovereignty over the exploration and exploitation of natural resources, including mineral resources and other non-living and non-living resources on the seabed and underwater. Other States may not unreasonably infringe on or interfere with the navigation or other rights of the coastal State.
Please be informed that the ancient maritime boundaries were the least accepted by the countries. In modern times, despite the laws that define maritime borders, maritime disputes are inevitable.
The 33rd President of the United States, Harry Truman, the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) defines the following maritime boundaries in accordance with UNCLOS.
