NASA scientists have discovered a new theory that explains the loss of water on Mars.
According to the above research, during its early period, Mars may have had water resources on its surface, about half the volume of the Earth's Atlantic Ocean (the Atlantic Ocean is storing 323 points). Million cubic kilometers).
The scientists also pointed out that there are three processes used in the new model to explain the loss of water on the surface of Mars: first: water loss due to volcanic activity, second: water loss. Into space and third: water lost (infiltration) into the crust of Mars.
