Japanese Government Provides More Than $ 3 Million to End Violence Against Children During Kovid 19 Outbreak
In response, the Japanese government and UNICEF are launching a new project called "Preventing and Responding to Violence against Children in Cambodia During the Global Outbreak of Kovid-19 and Rehabilitation."
The aim of the project is to ensure that boys and girls in Cambodia are protected from violence, exploitation and abuse during the Kovid-19 epidemic, and to rehabilitate and strengthen human security. Some child protection services were disrupted during the outbreak, so the project aims to ensure the rehabilitation and resumption of these child protection services.
The project will develop existing strategies and interventions to strengthen child protection services for vulnerable children and their families throughout Cambodia during the Kovid-19 outbreak and rehabilitation.
This project will support the Royal Government of Cambodia in establishing and implementing an institutional and legal framework to address the potential risk factors for Kovid-19 on child protection. The project will also support teachers, academic staff, parents and caregivers in learning new ways to increase their knowledge, attitudes and practices to prevent and respond to violence against children.
Child safety online will be enhanced by strengthening partnerships with the information and technology (ICT) industry and raising awareness and educational activities for children and carers. Finally, data management and evidence-building will be strengthened through improved reporting and quality of data related to violence against children.
The ultimate goal is to support the Royal Government of Cambodia in establishing an appropriate and sustainable coordination of child protection systems to respond to the worsening of Kovid-19 under the auspices of civil society organizations (CSOs). And other relevant partners.
"All forms of violence that children experience can lead to serious and lifelong consequences, and we are very concerned that they could be at high risk," said Ms. Ferguson Youssef, UNICEF Country Director for Cambodia. "In the face of violence and abuse during the difficult period of the Kovid-19 pandemic."
"We would like to thank the Government of Japan for its significant and timely contribution," she said. "This project aims to strengthen child protection systems and services and contribute to the achievement of the rights of Cambodian children to grow in a safe and protective environment."
In particular, Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia, said: "Childhood violence can cause many dangers to children and their lives. "Victims of childhood violence often lead to negative behavioral, psychological and physical effects in adolescents and adults, which is also a challenge for economic development."
