Please read the full decision below: APSARA Authority requires tourists visiting Angkor to follow the Angkor Code of Conduct and the guidelines of the Ministry of Health
(Siem Reap): The Apsara Authority, which is responsible for the conservation and development of the Angkor site, requires tourists visiting the Angkor site to follow the Angkor Code of Conduct and the guidelines of the Ministry of Health, as well as the recommendations of the head of the Royal Government. .
The Apsara Authority claims that from the 9th to the 15th century, Angkor was the famous former Khmer capital, nicknamed the Khmer Empire. To this day, Angkor is not only a monument for Buddhists and the general Cambodian people to worship, but also a unique cultural treasure of humankind in the whole world.
In particular, at a time when the virus 19 is spreading rapidly, the APSARA Authority requires all national and international tourists who visit the Angkor resort area to abide by the Angkor Code of Conduct and follow the guidelines of the Ministry of Health, such as wearing a mask. Keep a distance, wash your hands often and do not gather in large numbers or in dark places.
Therefore, respecting the Angkor Code of Conduct and following the guidelines of the Ministry of Health is helping to keep ourselves safe as well as contributing to the preservation and value of the Angkor resort area. Myanmar military charges Aung San Suu Kyi with corruption charges
Rangoon: Aung San Suu Kyi continues to face new charges from the Myanmar military, while her lawyer says those allegations are baseless, with the aim of making it impossible for her to return to politics. Yes. This was released by AFP on Thursday evening, March 18, 2021.
The Myanmar military has already charged Suu Kyi with a number of crimes since she and several of her top officials were detained during a February 1 coup. The charges include illegally possessing radio communication equipment (ICOM) and violating Kovid-19 measures, as well as bribery.
On Wednesday night, Myanmar military media released a video showing a Myanmar businessman confessing to bribing Suu Kyi for a total of $ 550,000, and authorities are preparing to charge her more under the anti-government law. Corruption. In the past, the Myanmar military has also charged her with several other corruption cases, accepting about $ 600,000 in cash and more than 10 kilograms of gold.
In connection with the allegations against Suu Kyi, her lawyer, Khin Maung Zaw, on Thursday told reporters that the allegations were baseless. All the basics are clear, but it is possible to tarnish her name and prevent her from participating in political activities.
