Cambodia's economy, meanwhile, is projected to fail to grow as much as 4 percent if projected Kovid 19 disease persists for a long time.
Mr. Chheng Kimlong made the remarks in an interview with "Special Guest of the Week" on "Economic Impacts of the 19 Kovid Outbreak in the Community on February 20".
Speaking in this interview, Mr. Chheng Kimlong said that the event on February 20 is a very interesting event because it has an impact and impact on public health, business, trade and economy.
He added that so far, due to the spread of Kovid 19 in some provinces and some areas in Phnom Penh, there has been a significant negative impact. Apparently, there is a strong impact on the business sector, in which some shops are closed, some shops start to reduce working hours and so on.
"These business disruptions have affected people's incomes, small and medium enterprises' incomes have affected family businesses," he added. It also affects the transport sector, tourism, the closure of businesses and restaurants to comply with the measures of the Royal Government of Cambodia to prevent the spread of the community and affect the health of the people of Cambodia.
He said that after closing some businesses, some areas were also affected. "The level of impact of the third 19 cases of Kovid or the February 20 community may be worse than the first and second incidents," he said.
He said that according to the forecast of the Ministry of Economy and Finance that in 2021, the Cambodian economy could grow by 4 percent, at least 3.5 percent. After the third outbreak of Kovid 19 outbreak, I think the economy in 2021 may not be as good as expected if the outbreak continues for another month and The number of infections continues to rise.
