This morning, the Minister of Health and the Ministry of Justice held a conference to clarify the issue of making a law on measures to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 and malignant diseases.
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Mr. Mam Bunheng, and the Minister of Justice, Mr. Keut Rith, will hold a joint press conference at 9 am on Monday, March 15, 2021 to clarify the establishment of the law on "measures to prevent infection. "Outbreaks of Kovid-19 and other deadly and dangerous diseases."
The conference was held to demonstrate the purpose and legitimacy of the enactment of the "Measures to Prevent the Spread of Kovid-19 and Other Deadly and Dangerous Infectious Diseases."
The Law on "Measures to Prevent the Outbreak of Kovid-19 and Other Deadly and Dangerous Infections" was officially promulgated on March 11, 2021. This law has 6 chapters and 18 articles.
This law is designed to determine the health measures, administrative measures and other measures to be taken to combat and prevent the spread of Kovid 19 and other deadly and serious infectious diseases aimed at protecting the lives of citizens, public health. And public order, as well as minimizing the impact of this infection on the social and economic sectors in Cambodia.
In this law, attention is paid to the level of fines and severe penalties for people as well as those who do not comply with the authorities' measures to fight and prevent infectious diseases or spread the disease to others. The fines range from 2 million riel to 20 million riel, and imprisonment ranges from six months to 20 years.
The law has also been criticized by some civil society organizations. They say the legislation puts an extra weight on freedom of expression.
Mr. Keut Rith had emphasized in front of the National Assembly that the law was designed to prevent and curb the spread of disease in the Cambodian community, especially to protect the lives of the people and public health.
"I would like to emphasize that this law is made for the sole purpose of protecting the lives of the people, protecting the public health of the people," he said. "As for the sentence, we generally say that the sentence was created to punish the corrupt and the wicked who want to endanger the lives of the people and public health."
Mr. Keut Rith added, "For people who follow the correct measures of the Royal Government of the Ministry of Health to ensure that there is no outbreak, our community can return to normal, this law does not apply. To them no. "This provision applies only to corrupt people, to corrupt people who intend to cause chaos in Cambodian society."
Responding to the human rights group that reacted, Mr. Keut Rith raised the question: Is it wrong to make laws to protect the lives of the people, to make laws to get rid of the wicked?
It should be noted that the 19 laws of Kovid, which came into force on March 11, impose severe penalties. Below are the levels of fines and imprisonment for those who violate the rules set by the authorities.
