General knowledge: A disease that claimed 50 million lives a century ago
In the early stages, the Spanish flu invaded Europe, the United States, and parts of Asia before spreading rapidly around the world. At that time, there was no drug or vaccine to treat this type of flu. People were ordered to wear masks, and schools, cinemas and businesses were closed as corpses clogged the morgue before the disease disappeared on its own.
The emergence of the Spanish flu, a new strain of influenza, began in early 1918 but did not appear to be severe. Most infected people only show mild flu symptoms, such as fever and fatigue, and recover in the next few days while the death toll is low.
According to experts, the cause of the Spanish flu massacre was due to the fact that the health sector at that time was still very weak, with no respiratory aids or means to develop a vaccine. In addition, measures were taken to prevent the spread of the disease, and the spread of the disease was less widespread, including during World War I.
The second epidemic was the deadliest from August to the end of 1918. The Spanish flu lasted until the mid-1920s, with the third and fourth epidemic before it disappeared. In the total period of the pandemic from 1918-1920, the flu killed between 20 and 50 million of the estimated 500 million infected people. This figure is an estimate because at that time there were not many accurate data records.
The Spanish flu epidemic ended in the mid-1920s, when the epidemic was no longer spontaneous and most infected people developed antibodies to protect themselves from the disease.
Since then, there have been a number of flu cases that have spread globally, but not as severely as the Spanish flu. An outbreak of influenza from 1957-1958 claimed the lives of 2 million people around the world and another from 1968-1969 killed about 1 million people.
