Recently, the General Department of Taxation organized a workshop on "Capital Gains Tax" to disseminate this tax to stakeholders.
Dr. Ken Sambath, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Taxation, stated that the capital gains tax is exempt from the sale or transfer of some capital, including:
1: Property owned by state institutions
2. Property belonging to foreign embassies or consulates, international organizations or technical cooperation agencies of other governments
3: The original residence of the taxpayer who occupied it for at least 5 years before the sale or transfer. In case there is more than one residence or the taxpayer and the spouse have different residences, only one residence is allowed as the original residence.
4. Real estate within the circle of relatives as stated in the provisions on stamp duty, except for the transfer of ownership or possession of real estate between siblings and siblings between parents-in-law and son-in-law and between grandmothers-in-law and grandchildren.
5. Property that is sold or transferred for public benefit in accordance with the law on expropriation.
He added that the Royal Government has decided to waive the tax on capital gains on the sale or transfer of agricultural land owned or occupied by people who are farmers who are actually growing crops and have a residence in the commune / sangkat where the agricultural land. That is there.
It should also be noted that the tax on capital gains is scheduled to be implemented from the beginning of 2022.
