General knowledge: How many natural resource protection areas does Cambodia currently have and what is the area of the country?
(Phnom Penh): Natural resource protection areas are public property lands of the state, including land or sea, including beaches and seas, within the areas recognized by the provisions of the law or newly created areas within the jurisdiction of the Ministry. An environment that ensures the protection of important species of wildlife and plants, as well as natural communities.
How many natural resource protection zones does Cambodia currently have and what is the total area of the whole country?
As we all know, Cambodia covers a total area of 181,035 square kilometers, 800 kilometers north-west of Thailand, 541 kilometers northeast of Laos, and 541 kilometers northeast and east. With Vietnam 1,228 km. The maritime border stretches over the Gulf of Thailand for 443 km.
Regarding the number of natural resource protection areas in Cambodia, Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that up to 2021, Cambodia has 73 natural resource protection areas with a total area of 7,297,314 hectares. And 41% of the total land area of Cambodia.
The 73 protected areas are in eight major protected areas:
These national parks are rich in rare species of wildlife and rich in dense rainforest and are also a major tourist attraction.
These wildlife sanctuaries are rich in endangered species, endangered species, endangered and endangered species, as well as home to many species of wildlife and biodiversity, especially rich in natural resources. Dense forest and swamp forest.
Third: Landscape area This landscape area is divided into 13 different areas and has a total area of 153,604 hectares, including Angkor resort area, Banteay Chhmar area, Preah Vihear area, Anlong Pring area, North Tonle Sap lowland area, Boeung Prek Lpou area, Ang Trapeang Thmor area, Bakan temple area. "Preah Khan, Kampong Svay", Ronam Daun Sam area, Sambo Prei Kuk area, Boeung Lom Kot area, Phnom Kraing Dey Meas area and Boeung Yak Om-Yak Kara area.
For this landscape area is rich in ancient temples, cultural heritage, forest and many other natural resources.
Fourth: Multi-use areas Multi-use areas are divided into 8 different areas and have a total area of 415,796 hectares, including Dang Prek area, Samlot area, Tonle Sap area, Ponhea Krek area, Prek Teuk Sap area, Kbal Chhay area, Phnom Neang Kangri-Phnom area. Golden Boat, Lion Pillar Area and Boeung Yak Laom Area.
Fifth: Ramsar area The Ramsar area is divided into 5 areas and covers a total area of 60,995 hectares, including Stung Treng, Koh Kapi, Tonle Chhmar, Prek Toal and Stung Sen areas.
Seventh: Natural Heritage Area This natural heritage area is divided into 11 different areas and covers a total area of 33,951 hectares, including Phnom Tbeng, Phnom Yat, Seven Mountains, Wall area, Kiriyong area, Phnom Tak Traing area, Phnom Preah Net Preah area. Phnom Preah - Phnom Veng, Phnom Yeay Sam, Phnom Bak and Phnom Svay.
Eighth: Marine Park Area This sea park area has a total area of 52,448 hectares, Koh Rong Sea area. In addition, there is the Boeung Prek Top Freshwater Conservation Area with a total area of 1,630 hectares and the Biodiversity Conservation Corridor with a total area of 142,940 hectares.
The establishment of these protected areas by the Royal Government is aimed at transforming the areas into mountainous natural heritage sites, enhancing the value of history, culture, biodiversity conservation, improving the livelihoods of the people and tourism in those areas.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that with good protection and conservation efforts, large foreign companies have purchased carbon credits from Cambodia. Since 2016, Cambodia has sold carbon credits in the voluntary market for $ 11.6 million to strengthen forest protection and conservation and develop local communities, create jobs and income for communities, build strong fences, protect areas. Protect nature as well.
