Cambodian, Laotian and Vietnamese leaders meet on video to discuss Kovid 19 and strengthen ties
(Phnom Penh): The Prime Minister of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Laos, Mr. Thongloun Sisoulit, and the Prime Minister of Vietnam, Mr. Nguyen Suon Phuc, met on Wednesday morning, March 10, 2021. On a number of key issues related to Kovid 19, the reopening of flights and the strengthening of relations between the three countries.
According to a press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Prime Ministers of Laos and Vietnam thanked the Prime Minister for the important initiative of the Prime Minister to hold a video meeting as a contribution. Strengthen and further expand the traditional friendship and fruitful cooperation of the three countries.
During the meeting, the three Prime Ministers briefed on the latest developments in their countries, including the fight against the spread of Kovid-19. The Prime Minister congratulated his Vietnamese and Lao counterparts on the success of the 13th General Assembly of the Communist Party of Vietnam, the 11th General Assembly of the Lao People's Revolutionary Party and the Lao National Assembly election.
The Prime Minister also praised Vietnam for its achievements in socio-economic development by 2020, despite the spread of Kovid-19 disease. Prime Minister Hun Sen also congratulated Laos and Vietnam on effectively controlling the spread of Kovid-19 disease.
The three Prime Ministers agreed to further strengthen cooperation and mutual support in combating the spread of Kovid-19. The three prime ministers also agreed to provide each other with free Kovid-19 vaccination to Cambodian diplomats and students from Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam who are staying in each country.
The three Prime Ministers expressed their mutual appreciation for the growing ties and cooperation in all fields between the three countries, despite the high risk of the Kovid-19 outbreak. The three Prime Ministers also noted with pleasure the increase in trade volume between Cambodia-Laos and Cambodia-Vietnam.
In the field of connectivity, the three Prime Ministers agreed to consider the resumption of international flights between Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam to boost trade and investment, as well as exchanges between the peoples of the three countries.
Under the sub-regional cooperation, the three Prime Ministers agreed to accelerate the implementation of the CLV Economic Connectivity Plan to 2030, focusing on cooperation in mobilizing resources from development partners to build the Hanoi-Vientiane Expressway. Vientiane - Vong An and Phnom Penh - Ho Chi Minh Railway.
In addition, the Prime Ministers of Vietnam and Laos reaffirmed their commitment to fully support Cambodia in hosting the 7th Mekong Sub-Region Summit in March 2021 at the ASEM Summit. 13th at the end of 2021 and ASEAN chairmanship in 2022.
At the same time, the three Prime Ministers agreed to assign the relevant ministries and institutions and all relevant provincial authorities to implement the agreements reached in the video meeting, as well as to enhance friendship and multifaceted cooperation between the two countries. Three Cambodia - Laos and Vietnam.
The three leaders also exchanged views on regional and international issues of common interest and concern.
The meeting comes amid a number of unresolved issues, including the deadlock over nuclear diplomacy with Pyongyang and the delay in the transfer of total operational control (OPCON) from the United States. Back to South Korea.
The agenda at the forthcoming meeting could include the creation of a new impetus to engage with North Korea and push forward the stalled nuclear disarmament talks, as well as expedite the transfer of control. Operation Opson (OPCON) as well as the resilience of the Allies to address global issues such as climate change and cyber threats.
On the occasion of the visit of senior US officials, Seoul and Washington may also sign a formal agreement to share the cost of the 28,500 US troops stationed in South Korea also to show real intention to strengthen the alliance.
