The following are the instructions of the NEC: Letter to readers: Preservation of ancient Buddhist architecture at Wat Lanka, Phnom Penh
(Phnom Penh): In Cambodia, there are many architectural structures left over, including pre-Angkorian heritage buildings, Angkorian period, post-Angkorian period, a century-old colonial architecture from 1863 to 1953, and architecture Modern.
Due to the development of technology and architecture, multi-storey and high-rise buildings have grown rapidly in Cambodia, while heritage buildings such as public administration buildings, religious buildings, residential buildings have declined and have increased. Modifications are different from the original. These ancient heritage buildings are closely linked to the life, culture, religion, society, economy and history of the Cambodian people.
Samdech Preah Moha Areyvong, Dr. Saor Chanthol, Third Vice President of the Buddhist Assembly in the Kingdom of Cambodia and Chief Bishop of Wat Lanka, Kosomaram, said he was pleased that Buddhists were helping to restore ancient Buddhist structures in In Wat Lanka, Buddhists will receive merits. He expressed regret over the ancient architecture, which is evidence that has long since been lost.
Sun Pora, a student of architecture and head of the Khmer pagoda architecture project, said that ancient Buddhist architecture had to be protected because the pagoda was badly damaged during the war during the Khmer Republic and the Khmer Rouge regime. To date, only a handful of ancient Buddhist architecture remains.
