According to the United Nations, Myanmar security forces last week cracked down and killed more than 50 people since the events of February 1, known internationally as a military coup. According to human rights groups, nearly 1,800 people have been arrested by Myanmar military authorities.
In response to the deadly protests, the United States and some Western countries have imposed sanctions on the Myanmar military, while on Sunday, Australia announced the severance of military ties with Myanmar and diverted aid to Myanmar. Holds NGOs as well.
Promoting dialogue may be more effective than punishing. In the aftermath of the coup, the United States immediately announced sanctions on Myanmar's military regime, and the European Union agreed to join. But no matter how hard these Western countries impose sanctions, it is unlikely to change the military or resolve the crisis in Myanmar, because in the end the worst victims are still civilians. .
He also stressed that the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, or ASEAN, could act as an arbiter to bring Aung San Suu Kyi's Myanmar military and National League for Democracy back to the negotiating table and help them achieve their goals. A consensus on the sharing of power.
* The role of ASEAN
ASEAN should play a constructive role. ASEAN has the principle of non-interference in the internal affairs of other member countries and the principle of consensus or consensus. But this does not mean that ASEAN has to stand by and watch the crisis in Myanmar. Experts believe that while ASEAN can work on the principle of consensus, ASEAN can also cooperate on the principle of group pressure as a friend to persuade the Myanmar military, as they did in 2005.
In addition to these principles, ASEAN adheres to many other principles, including democracy, the rule of law, good governance and respect for human rights. In fact, ASEAN has always played an important role in promoting democracy in Myanmar. In 2005, ASEAN succeeded in pressuring the Myanmar military government to relinquish its chairmanship in 2006 to focus on accelerating Myanmar's national reconciliation and democracy.
China can play a constructive role because, as we know, China and Myanmar have a special friendship. In a recent interview, Chinese Ambassador to Myanmar Chen Hai claimed that China was unaware of the changes in the political situation in Myanmar, and he categorically denied the allegations. It is reported that China is transporting aid by air to the Myanmar military.
ASEAN is one of the major organizations in the region, of which Myanmar is a direct member, and China is a neighbor of Myanmar. If ASEAN and China can really work together with Myanmar and aim to resolve the current crisis, it is possible that the hot situation in Myanmar will cool down peacefully soon, even if the long-term solution is on Myanmar itself.
