On the occasion of International Women's Day, March 8! Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh Sends Funds and Food to 148 Deaf Children in Siem Reap
(Siem Reap): Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of National Defense and Chairman of the Siem Reap Provincial Assistance Team, assigned Mr. Tea Seiha, Governor of Siem Reap Province, to provide budget and some food items to 148 deaf children in Siem Reap. Siem Reap Orphanage in Siem Reap Special Education High School on the occasion of the 110th anniversary of International Women's Day, March 8, 2021.
The ceremony was attended by Mr. Seang Nam, Member of Parliament of Siem Reap Province, Mr. An Khun, Chairman of Siem Reap Provincial Council, Ms. Teang Eng Tea Seiha, Vice President of Siem Reap Provincial Red Cross Branch, Leaders of Institutions around the Siem Reap Red Cross Youth Federation. Provinces, teachers, deaf children, children with disabilities as well.
Siem Reap Special Education High School was established on October 1, 2000 by Krousar Thmey and from December 29, 2017 until now, this high school is managed by the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports. Currently, the high school has a total of 39 staff (21 females) and a total of 183 students (74 females) in the new school year 2020-2021.
It should be noted that the management provided food and snacks to 222 deaf and disabled children, a total of nearly 9 million riels, and provided 1325 notebooks, 491 pens, 222 student bags, water bottles. 1 Sarong 224, Krama 224, 31 million Riels for all children and 45 teachers.
In addition, 5 tons of rice, 5 cases of canned fish, 20 cases of noodles, 10 cases of orange juice were provided for Siem Reap Special Education High School with 10 bicycles, 6 fans and 3 wardrobes, and for Siem Reap Orphanage 7 closets as well.
Samdech Techo Urges the Implementation of 7 Priorities to Ensure the Promotion of Women's Rights
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on the occasion of International Women's Day, March 8, highlighted the many achievements of the Royal Government of Cambodia to promote rights and provide opportunities for women to participate in social work in line with the slogan " "Women are the backbone of the national economy."
In his message on the occasion of the 110th International Women's Day, March 8, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that the Royal Government has taken care to increase the proportion of women in public institutions so that women can be more involved in leading and acting. Decisions at both the national and sub-national levels have significantly increased the number of women in the legislature from one mandate to another.
In the executive branch, there is an increase in women as Deputy Prime Minister, Minister, Secretary of State and Undersecretary of State. In the field of civil service, the number of female civil servants increased from 35% in 2007 to 41% by 2020, and the proportion of female civil servants in decision-making positions increased to 24% in 2019.
In the economic field, women are actively involved and have the most economic opportunities. Women make up 79% of the workforce and 61% of business owners (mostly micro, small and medium enterprises).
Sakru Chet lawyers, Samdech Techo Sen's lawyers, have been involved in providing legal services to poor women victims of violence in all 25 capitals and provinces.
For social protection and health, the maternal mortality rate of children under 1 year of age and children under 5 years of age has dropped significantly. Cambodia is one of the top 10 countries in the world to achieve key indicators related to maternal and child health, including preventing the spread of HIV and AIDS ahead of the Cambodian Millennium Development Goals.
Second, expand the comprehensive coverage of the social protection system, including the well-being of women and girls, with the implementation of all social assistance programs.
Third, continue to strengthen the economic empowerment of women through increased awareness and business development for women, the development of micro, small and medium enterprises (MSME), strengthen vocational training programs and the financial environment.
Fourth, develop human and digital capital, especially for women and girls, by increasing investment in education and skills development in digital technology and education in science, technology, engineering, creative arts and mathematics (STEAM).
Fifth, continue to ensure the equal protection of women's rights so that women can make decisions at all levels, help address all barriers that discriminate against women in decision-making in public institutions at all levels and in politics.
Sixth: Promote social morality, values of women and Cambodian families by strengthening the implementation of positive parenting programs, a culture of non-violence and increasing investment in the implementation of the National Action Plan on the Prevention of Violence against Women 2019-2023. Both national and sub-national levels.
Seventh, strengthen the accountability mechanism for gender equality of ministries and institutions at all levels through the development of capacity and effective monitoring and evaluation system and transparency. The Ministry of Health has rejected a newspaper report saying that 19 undocumented Kovid workers returning from Thailand have been exempted from conspiracy.
