(Phnom Penh): Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi, Minister of Women's Affairs, issued a letter of thanks to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, for delivering a message to celebrate the 110th anniversary of International Women's Day, March 8, 2021 for broadcast on the media. Various.
The Ministry of Women's Affairs emphasized that the Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen's noble gesture expressed his full support for the spirit and means of promoting the implementation of the National Policy on Gender Equality in Cambodia to contribute to accelerating development. Sustainability and environment.
The following is a letter of thanks from Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi: General Knowledge: Before International Women's Day, many tragedies occurred, please understand the history of this day.
The idea of demanding women's rights emerged during the Industrial Revolution. In Paris, France, many women called for freedom, equality and fraternity, as they marched on Versailles to demand the right to vote for women.
Unable to bear the pain of this oppression, it was not until March 8, 1899, that the textile and garment workers in Chicago and New York, United States, united to stand firm. Require employers to reduce working hours and increase salaries. But unfortunately, these workers were increasingly oppressed and were arrested, tortured and fired from their factories.
Despite the pain and danger, the workers remained united and continued to protest in public again and again to force the factory owners to agree to the workers' request. The struggle of workers in the United States has had a profound effect on women workers around the world.
The following is a brief history of events leading up to International Women's Day:
1910 In 1910, the International Socialist Association convened in Copenhagen, Denmark, to mark International Women's Day with the aim of commemorating the voting movement and its activities. The meeting was attended by 100 women delegates from 17 countries, but no specific date has been set for the day.
In 1917, an estimated 2 million women in the Russian military chose to strike on the last Sunday of February to demand food and peace. Russian political leaders have opposed the timing of the strike, but the military has not ruled out a strike.
Four days later, the Russian Tsar was forced to abdicate, and the caretaker government granted women the right to vote. The historic Sunday took place on February 23 according to the Julian calendar, which was then used in Russia, but compared to the Gregorian calendar used today, it is March 8.
1975 In 1975, the United Nations organized the First World Conference of Women in Mexico City, Mexico under the theme "Equality, Progress and Peace." The action plan reached at the time called for the elimination of all forms of discrimination against women. In October 1975, the United Nations announced that the celebration of International Women's Day would be held on March 8 every year.
1980 The Second World Conference on Women was held in Copenhagen, Denmark in 1980 to review the progress and implementation of the action plan set out at the first conference and to draw up a discussion line. Education, employment and health.
If we compare the history of International Women's Day on March 8 with the birth of the United Nations, we see that International Women's Day preceded the United Nations and Human Rights Day, while International Women's Day was established in 1911. Established in 1945, Human Rights Day was established in 1948.
