(Prey Veng): Rice traders at the Banteay Chakrei international border crossing in Preah Sdach district, Prey Veng province, have asked the Ministry of Skills and authorities to facilitate rice paddies on Cambodian soil to reduce costs while on the rice paddies. In Vietnam, they spend a lot of money, which makes them less profitable.
Vendors also learned that most of the rice brought to sell at Banteay Chakrei came from Pursat, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey, Kampong Thom and was lost for a day or three, causing the quality of his rice to be reduced by Vietnamese traders. Too much loss. Let the authorities facilitate the rice trucks to enter according to the order so that the mango trucks do not come later but turn first.
However, the traders request the Royal Government and the Prey Veng provincial authorities, as well as the relevant ministries, to facilitate the cross-border Vietnamese traders to buy rice at Ben in Cambodian territory so that Cambodian workers have jobs. And they also cut costs.
According to Khieu Hean, director of the Banteay Chakrei International Border Immigration Gate, Banteay Chakrei is rich in agricultural products such as rice and mangoes. And after the Chinese New Year, there is a significant increase in rice exports, and for mango, there is a season of two or three months a year, rice is always there.
As for traffic congestion at this port, your authority has taken care to facilitate the smooth export of our agricultural products, but there are also some congestion due to the large number of rice trucks from 150 to 250 units per 1 One day, the Vietnamese side allowed only five vehicles to enter at a time. As for mango goods, he facilitated them because they could not be stored for a long time, so please, all rice traders, do not be dissatisfied.
According to Chattalysak officials stationed at the Banteay Chakrei International Border Crossing, as health officials sprayed disinfectants on vehicles entering and exiting the gates and asked Tai Kong to wash his hands with alcohol and measure his temperature. According to the guidelines of the Ministry of Health to participate in the fight against Kovid-19 disease to prevent cases through this channel.
It should be noted that although Kovid-19 has erupted and forced the closure of the border between Cambodia and Vietnam, economic relations between the two countries are still normal.
In fact, for the Banteay Chakrei international border checkpoint, there are still normal exports and imports of goods, including goods imported from Vietnam, such as fish, vegetables, lobster, meat and spices. As for goods exported from Cambodia to Vietnam, there are only two types: mango and rice.
