Permanent Mission of Cambodia: Special Rapporteur's Assessment Misleads Right from Wrong
(Geneva): The Permanent Mission of the Kingdom of Cambodia in Geneva deeply regrets the release of information by four Special Rapporteurs on 5 March 2021 regarding the implementation of the law and the trial. Case in Cambodia.
In this statement, the Permanent Mission of the Kingdom of Cambodia emphasized that the Special Rapporteur's personal views were one-sided and politicized, as well as ignoring the facts and misleading the public. From right to wrong.
In this statement, the Cambodian Permanent Mission to the United Nations stated that the above experts had served in their own capacity and were not UN officials, having appointed themselves as judges for internal affairs. In the court of a sovereign state.
The statement added that the Special Rapporteur must fulfill his role strictly in accordance with the "Code of Conduct and Operational Manual for Special Procedure Holders", emphasizing the importance of complying with local regulations and the UN Charter. National.
In the statement, the Cambodian representative to the United Nations said that Professor Rona Smith seemed to have forgotten that she had personally condemned Sam Rainsy at the Human Rights Council in 2019 for defamation and incitement to overthrow the elected government. Appointed by Cambodians through elections. This is the same crime that Sam Rainsy and his cronies were found guilty of.
The statement added that the claim that the trial was not held in public and in the absence of the defendants further demonstrated that the Special Rapporteurs relied entirely on unverified sources and that the experts There seems to be little understanding or unwillingness to understand Cambodia's court proceedings.
The fact that defense lawyers representing foreign diplomats and civil society organizations were present in the courtroom, which shows that the court proceedings were conducted in a participatory and transparent manner. In all democracies, the decision to enter a courtroom is always at the discretion of the court, taking into account the actual size of the room and the orderly and secure conduct of the hearing.
