Pope Benedict XVI arrives in Baghdad, Iraq, on Friday for the most dangerous foreign trip since he was elected in 2013. According to a release by CNA.
Iraq has deployed thousands more security guards to protect the 84-year-old pope during a visit following a rocket attack and suicide bombing that raised concerns for his safety.
"I am delighted to be flying again ... This is a symbolic visit and it is obligatory to go there," Pope Francis told reporters aboard the plane. "Holds land that has been abandoned for many years."
During his historic visit, Pope Francis will address a crowd at a mosque in Baghdad and meet with a Shiite cleric in the southern Iraqi city of Najaf. The prince will then travel north of Mosul.
It should be noted that this visit to Iraq is the first visit of Samdech outside Italy since November 2019.
