APSARA Authority excavates at Veal Phtei temple near Beng Mealea temple to find an important secret
(Siem Reap): The Apsara Authority has started excavating at Veal Phtei temple, a small temple in the middle of the baray near Beng Mealea temple in Beng Mealea village, Beng Mealea commune, Svay Leu district, Siem Reap province, to collect a large piece of broken stone. Buried there and will reassemble the stone fragments as well as to examine the possibility of restoring the temple.
The ceremony was held on the afternoon of March 4, 2021 in the presence of Mr. Kim Sothin, Deputy Director General of APSARA Authority and Mr. Mao Si, President of Banteay Chhmar Tourism Community, on behalf of Ms. Mao Malay Ke Kim Yan and colleagues. Apsara Local Authority as well.
Please be informed that Beng Mealea Temple was built in the late 11th and early 12th centuries during the reign of King Suryavarman II in the Angkorian style to dedicate to Vishnu in Brahmanism. All the temples dedicated to Vishnu always have statues of Vishnu and other deities in various postures, in which the image of Vishnu resting on the eternal dragon is also highlighted.
Therefore, if the Veal Phtei temple was built under the handiwork of King Suryavarman II, then the suspected stone carving may be more than the image of Vishnu resting on the dragon.
