Nine Decade-Korki Trees in Romeas Primary School in Kampong Chhnang Province Destroyed and Sold for 12 Million Riel Criticized
(Kampong Chhnang): Recently, the case of cutting down 9 Koki trees that are more than 6 decades old, which have been planted since the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era in Bun Rany Hun Sen Romeas Primary School in Sre Ta Chey Village, Aphivath Commune, Teuk Phos District, Kampong Chhnang Province. Kampong Chhnang cut and sold for a total of 12 million riel to raise money to build a school has been strongly criticized by the public.
The case has sparked widespread criticism from government officials, the public and the public, who say school leaders should not cut down the decades-old Koki tree. There are nine trees in Koki and two trees have been cut down. The case that there is a lack of funds to build the school until the trees are cut down to raise money to repair the school does not seem reasonable at all, because the big trees that are planted in public places are private property of the state.
Responding to the above criticism, Mr. Khim Phalla, Director of the Teuk Phos District Education Office, answered by phone that the nine Koki trees were planted in front of a kindergarten building, which is only 4 meters in front of the school building.
In particular, during 2015, this Koki branch used to fall to the ground, crushing the students' bicycles and the swing that the kindergarten students were riding on, and it was also damaged. At that time, if a kindergarten student falls, is injured or dies, then it is also a big responsibility of the principal and teachers. Later, the school also cut down eight Koki branches that grow in front of the school building to avoid the risk of accidents.
