International Analysis: Two US Standards for the International Criminal Court
The State Department immediately criticized the ICC decision to investigate Israel's actions. The United States is hot-tempered after Bensouda said on March 3 that she had launched a formal investigation into war crimes in the Palestinian territories, which would be a test of her determination. Biden to protect human rights.
* Two US standards
In fact, the United States does not have good relations with the ICC. In 2000, former US President Bill Clinton signed but did not ratify the Rome Statute to become a member of the ICC, fearing that his army or citizens would be prosecuted for war crimes while the United States went to war. Around the world.
In March 2002, former President George W. Bush announced that the United States would not ratify the Rome Statute, and both houses of Congress passed a law barring all states or federal agencies from cooperating. Operates with courts that prosecute against American citizens. Such a position on the US ICC remains to this day.
Since its inception in 2002, most ICCs have prosecuted or prosecuted individuals or former leaders in Africa, as they have not been able to prosecute or prosecute individuals from superpowers. Or the West. Of the 13 cases currently under investigation by the ICC, 10 are in Africa.
