Mr. Chin Malin: Law Enforcement of Criminals is Not a Restriction of Democracy (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Mr. Chin Malin, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Justice on March 3, 2021 That the space for democracy in Cambodia is always open for those who exercise their rights and freedoms in accordance with the Constitution and the law. While enforcing the law against perpetrators is not a restriction on democracy.
Mr. Sam Rainsy and eight other party members, including: Mr. Eng Chhay Eang, Ms. Mu Sochua, Mr. Ou Chanrith, Mr. Ho Van, Mr. Long Ry, Mr. Men Sothavarin, Ms. Chulong Soumura and Mr. Nut Rumduol on the 26th In September 2019, the Phnom Penh Municipal Court charged him with "murder" in connection with an attempted coup to overthrow the legitimate government under a plot to return to Cambodia during the November 9, 2019 incident.
In response to the US ambassador's remarks, Jean Malin said: "The application of the law to perpetrators is not a restriction on democracy. "Criticism of court procedures and decisions is not based on the legal basis and legal procedures of Cambodia, and in order to protect the perpetrators, it is an unjust and double standard critique."
Around this point, Mr. Chin Malin also mentioned three points of legal knowledge about the events of November 9, those involved and legally responsible for Sam Rainsy and eight other accomplices, including:
First, the plan for Sam Rainsy's return on November 9, 2019, was a plot to overthrow the legitimate government that emerged from the election, especially to oust the legitimate Prime Minister through incitement. The armed forces and civil servants to revolt in the form of violent revolutions and the creation of funds to carry out the coup. These acts are subversive, the most serious crimes affecting national security.
And third, in principle, those involved in the November 9 incident must participate in court proceedings and exercise their right to legal protection with the assistance of a lawyer to acquit and challenge the charges. This is the only legal way to defend oneself from charges in a democratic society, the rule of law, not to demand pressure from the courts, which are independent institutions, to release or drop charges.
In addition to convicting Sam Rainsy, the court also sentenced Mu Sochua and Eng Chhay Eang to 22 years in prison and Chou Long Somura, Men Sotheavrin, Ou Chanrith, Ho Van, Long Ry and Nuth Romduol. He was also sentenced to 20 years in prison.
