(ASEAN): Although in the 19th century almost all of Asia fell under the yoke of European colonization, but Britain and France left a kingdom as a buffer zone between their colonies without competition. Do not control that. Where is the buffer zone between the two major colonies in the country and what are the reasons for the country to break free from the yoke of colonization?
In 1498, the Portuguese navy invaded the Indian state of Goa and crossed the Indian Ocean to Southeast Asia to invade the Malacca Peninsula in 1511 AD.
The presence of these three colonial armies did not seem to be very competitive and did not go deep into the kingdoms on the mainland. Most of their location is along the coast. It was not until the arrival of British and French troops that arrived in Asia later in the 18th century that colonial events on the continent began to escalate.
Meanwhile, French armies arrived in Indochina in 1858 and colonized Cambodia, the Tonkin Kingdom, the Annamite Kingdom, and Kampuchea Krom, later occupying the Lao Kingdom of Laos from Siam and parts of China. .
Collectively, in the early 20th century, before World War I, the territories of India and Southeast Asia fell under the yoke of European colonization almost entirely, except for Siam, the only country that could escape the clutches of the West. The United Kingdom and France agreed in 1896, leaving the Siamese Empire as a buffer zone or "white zone" between the two colonies.
