Mr. Ouk Vibol, Director of the Department of Fisheries Conservation, said: "I highly appreciate the participation of local authorities, local communities, people and especially WCS, which has been rehabilitating this endangered species. This natural water is restored.
At the same time, Mr. Vibol also asked the relevant parties to continue the work of restoring this species of turtle and also reminded some perpetrators who continue to trade this species will be punished before the law.
It is one of the 25 endangered species of freshwater and terrestrial turtles in the world. This species of turtle is listed on the IUCN Red List as the most endangered species and is considered a reptile species representing Cambodia, as defined in the 2005 Royal Decree.
Due to illegal fishing, over-exploitation, habitat loss and sand mining in the Prek Sre Ambel system in Koh Kong and Sihanoukville provinces have affected the turtle habitat and breeding. It is found only in the Prek Sre Ambel system in Koh Kong and Sihanoukville provinces in Cambodia. These turtles are facing extinction.
Since then, WCS and the Fisheries Administration have worked together to protect the species from extinction. These turtle conservation activities include protection of turtle nests, rearing after hatching, prevention of illegal trade, enforcement of law enforcement, monitoring, promotion and support of community livelihoods under the financial support of the owners. Many donors include the European Union and the Singapore Wildlife Park (WRS).
Ken Serey Ratha, Director of WCS, stated: “The turtle nest protection program plays an important role in protecting these rare and endangered species by encouraging the participation of local communities in "Protect and maintain the turtle nest until the baby turtle hatches successfully." Ministry of Agriculture and Administration of Kampot Province Jointly Conserve Mangrove Forest After Some Areas Are Destroyed and Invaded
(Kampot): Mr. Has Sareth, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Mr. Chiao Tay, Governor of Kampot Province, chaired a meeting on fisheries conservation and management of coastal mangrove forests on the afternoon of March 1, 2021. Attended by accompanying delegates, officials and relevant authorities.
The purpose of this meeting was to focus on discussing and presenting specific results reports on the current state of the remaining mangrove forest in Kampot province.
According to the discussion at the meeting, officials from the Ministry of Agriculture and the Kampot Provincial Administration were very concerned about the loss of the mangrove forest, which is a biodiversity sanctuary, and gave various reasons for the loss. The area of mangrove forest in Kampot province as well as the coastal provinces has not yet been clearly defined or demarcated.
In particular, almost half of the mangrove forest is in the salt fields owned by the people, while the mangrove forest along the coast has been flooded by individuals and companies for further illegal development, which makes the mangrove forest. Finding almost complete loss without timely prevention, control and conservation measures.
Mr. Danh Sary, Director of Kampot Provincial Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral Affairs, said that Kampot has great potential and has attracted many investment projects, especially in the coastal areas related to mangrove forests, which have led to encroachment and dumping. Subsequent landslides and the loss of many hectares of mangrove forest.
Sar Surin, director of the Kampot Provincial Fisheries Administration, confirmed that "almost half of the mangrove forest is in the rice fields for the production of salt, which is controlled by the people, so the fisheries can not control or set up border posts to protect and conserve."
Mr. Sok Kim Choeun, Director of the Department of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation also said that Kampot-Kep province has more than 900 hectares of rice fields, but some rice fields do not grow mangroves due to abandonment of salt production.
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture also requested and requested the Kampot Provincial Administration to urge professional officers to jointly inspect the actual location and map the demarcation of the mangrove forest quickly and clearly, and to demarcate the demarcation of the development zone in a timely manner. Mangroves may be completely lost for the benefit of future generations.
