Angkor residents using western baray water in agriculture enjoy increased rice yields over last year
(Siem Reap): Farmers of the Western Baray Irrigation Farmer Community are enjoying this season's rice yield, which is claimed to be higher than last year due to the influx of water from the Western Baray. Managed by the APSARA Authority on time and in sufficient premises to fill their dozens of hectares of rice fields.
The head of the Western Baray Irrigation Farmers' Community, Mr. Sok Mok, said that the yield of the farmers who received the Western Baray water this year was good. He added that the result is due to the water from the top down, easy and fast.
Community leader Mr. Sok Mak expressed his great pleasure to the APSARA Authority, which has always cooperated to provide water to farmers for irrigating rice according to actual needs.
For the community of farmers who use the water of the western baray irrigation system, Mr. Tim Chhom explained that this community has been established for a long time, but in 2014, the Royal Government handed over control to the APSARA Authority and continued to organize distribution work to farmers. Better yet.
However, officials from the Water Management Department said that after the water supply was met, farmers expanded their farmland, which resulted in a steady increase in water supply. Mr. Tim Chhom explained that before the management of the APSARA Authority, the total area of farmers' total farmland was only 13,000 hectares, but later the area of dryland rice was increased up to 2 times.
In this regard, APSARA Authority calls on all farmers to cultivate rice only once a year and to grow other mixed crops instead to reduce the risk of dependence on baray water, because now baray water has many functions. Sometimes the delivery of water to the people is not as required. Seek honors, have drugs, bring disaster! Nationwide drug crackdown for March 1: 9 cases cracked, 15 arrested
