O'Svay Dam in Kampong Speu Province Becomes a Popular Tourist Site
(Kampong Speu): The new resort of O'Svay Dam, a reservoir located in Srong commune, Kong Pisey district, Kampong Speu province, has become a tourist destination and attracts people from various areas to visit.
O'Svay Dam was established in 2016 to store water for irrigating people's fields during the dry season. But because of the vast expanse of water, it has become a spectacular sight and has attracted more and more tourists from year to year.
After frequent visitors come and visit here, the village-commune authorities also set up a place to relax, visit the water, set up food stalls and set up a place to play. Authorities also plan to promote the O'Svay Dam area into an easy-to-manage eco-tourism community.
Mr. Ros Sok, Acting Director of Kampong Speu Provincial Department of Tourism, said that there are a total of 18 tourist sites in Kampong Speu province, 5 tourist sites, 8 natural tourism sites and 5 agro-tourism sites.
He said that these areas have been popular to attract tourists to visit a lot of people, especially on weekends.
According to Ros Sok, the tourist attraction of the O'Svay Dam has also increased the income of the community.
He claimed that people living in O'Svay and Sla villages in Srong commune, Kong Pisey district earned income from selling food, drinks, renting boats, renting boats, renting water bags, renting car tires, sending bicycles, motorbikes, cars and other services.
Mr. Ros Sok also mentioned that the O'Svay Dam tourist area has contributed to improving the livelihood of the people in that area and helped reduce a large number of migrations.
You can go to O'Svay Dam on Road 143 and about 7 km from Kong Pisey District Hall.
The Acting Director of Kampong Speu Provincial Department of Tourism added that the village, commune and tourism authorities of the province are preparing to renovate the O'Svay Btao Dam as a place to relax, absorb fresh air and eat delicious food. Play with potential in the country. Spokesman for the General Commission: 460 more vehicles were inspected and fined on February 28, 2021
