Senior trade and agriculture official claims cashews are not easy to lose market despite strong reliance on overseas markets
(Phnom Penh): Currently, cashew crops are attracting a lot of interest from farmers because cashews are in a good market, but this market is completely dependent on foreign markets, which is a concern when they stop ordering. Buying will cause Cambodia to lose market share.
Speaking to Fresh News, Pen Sovicheat, Undersecretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said that the cashew market in Cambodia will not be easily lost and the likelihood of this happening is slim.
Mr. Pen Sovichet said that in general, the markets of neighboring countries can not be completely closed indiscriminately on any commodity, except in the case of scientific evidence or specific economic evidence that can affect the domestic market. That country.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce emphasized that Cambodia was looking to export to its target market without going through intermediaries.
Mr. Pen Sovicheat also said that the Ministry of Commerce is preparing a policy to promote the trade in cashew products with the participation of other relevant inter-ministerial ministries to promote production, ensure sustainability, supply chain and increase capacity.
In addition, to increase processing efficiency, diversify cashew products and find more markets through regional agreements, bilateral free trade agreements for the export of Cambodian products as a whole, especially agricultural products, including cashews. Also.
Mr. Ngin Chhay, the Royal Government delegate in charge of the General Department of Agriculture of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that the Ministry of Agriculture also has a plan to solve this problem to prevent this from happening.
Mr. Ngin Chhay said that by increasing investment in the construction of infrastructure to support the processing of cashew nuts in Cambodia to be able to absorb the results of cashew processing.
In addition, it must cooperate with all relevant ministries and institutions to find markets through the demonstration of the quality of Cambodian cashews in order to receive orders from the international market. This was further confirmed by Mr. Ngin Chhay, a senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture.
