Japan provides $ 2.9 million for Mekong River level monitoring and forecasting
The Mekong River and its tributaries provide livelihoods, food security and rich ecosystem services to an estimated 70 million people living along the Lower Mekong. The problem of low water flow in the Lower Mekong River in 2019 and 2020 has affected many countries along the Lower Mekong River, many of which have disrupted people's livelihoods.
Speaking at the signing ceremony held at the MRC Secretariat headquarters in Vientiane, Dr. An Pich Hattada, Executive Director of the MRC Secretariat, emphasized: "This new grant is centered on the issues facing our region. Encountered and expected to face in the future.
This grant is for a period of 4 years from this year to 2024. The budget is expected to increase capacity to monitor and evaluate the river environment and adapt to the general changes of the MRC and its member countries, including Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam.
Key activities supported under this assistance include the improvement of river monitoring networks and forecasting capabilities, as well as the establishment of new monitoring stations along the Mekong River. The funds will also be used to improve public information on floods and droughts through the use of modern technology, ensuring that local communities and governments have access to timely information to implement mitigation mechanisms. Appropriate.
The new grant is in addition to the $ 3.9 million that Japan provided to the MRC last year to improve flood and drought forecasting systems. As a long-term development partner of the MRC, Japan has provided more than $ 21 million in assistance to support flood and drought management, irrigation, climate change and environmental management.
