Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, urges people not to disturb these wild elephants and to participate in the protection and conservation. In Cambodia, there are between 400 and 600 wild elephants living in protected areas in Cambodia, including the northeastern region, which includes Mondulkiri and Ratanakkiri provinces, is rich in wild elephants. The number of domestic elephants is estimated at 71.
The Asian elephant is endangered throughout its habitat globally and is listed as endangered by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List. Habitat degradation has fragmented Asian elephants and negatively impacted the long-term stability of this important species.
Mr. Pheaktra continued that according to the study report in the Cardamom Mountains, there have been no reports of incidents of illegal elephant hunting since 2003.
