Samdech Techo Hun Sen departed from Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen on June 20, 1977 to flee to Vietnam in search of support for the movement to defeat the genocidal regime of Pol Pot. This distance is at a time when Samdech Kittipritthbandit is blessing his 5-month-old son, Mr. Hun Manet.
On the anniversary of the reunion of his wife and son, Samdech Techo Prime Minister, a leader with a bitter history in the Khmer background, highlighted the event and expressed his excitement and excitement. Excited to meet a wife who was thought to have died during the genocide, and emotional because her son did not know his father and called him uncle for about two months.
On this happy family reunion, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also commemorated the tragedy of the lives of Samdech and Samdech Kittiprittbandit during the time of the war of more than four decades ago.
In the memoir of the reunion with his wife and children, Samdech Techo also mentioned that 42 years ago, Samdech had only three members. But now Samdech has up to 34 family members, including Samdech and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, 5 children, 5 daughters-in-law and 22 grandchildren.
Samdech Techo also highlighted that this is also a reflection of the increase in population from about 5 million people in 1979 to about 16 million people now.
During the trip, I tried to ask the commander again to take care of my wife. But the same request, the same answer, which makes me almost unbearable to pull out this nasty gun, but patience is necessary to ensure survival for a struggle it has already set up some secret network. Come on.
At around 4 pm on November 11, 1976, when the motorbike arrived at the hospital, I stopped the motorbike without asking for permission because I was already a rider. I walked to the room where my wife was resting and saw Rany swollen. Since Rany was already a midwife, he told me that there could be a placenta that the doctor could not remove.
Despite the hardships, this is the first time I have lived with Rany for more than a week after marriage. In January 1977, Rany became pregnant again, which was against my wishes because I should not have children in such circumstances. I was ready to beat Pol Pot while my wife was pregnant. If a fight broke out, what about my wife and children?
I want to live with my wife, but I do not want to see my wife and children in danger when there is a fight. I asked the unit's political director to send Rany to work at the Army Hospital, located in Samrong village, Dar commune, Memot district, more than 30 kilometers away from my unit.
The breakup on June 20, 1977 was a breakup with little hope of reunion. Tens of millions of tears flowed with indescribable pain, but it was these tears that strengthened my spirit to strive for the survival of the Cambodian people. (The history of the national liberation struggle is well documented and is not necessary for me to write in the article regarding the reunion between me and my wife).
One thing I should remember is that around mid-February 1979, I had a dream that my wife Rany was on the other side of the creek where the deep water could not walk to each other. Waking up at 5 am woke me up from a dream. I woke up, I sat crying and could not find an explanation for this dream that Rany was alive or dead.
On the evening of February 24, 1979, while I was working for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs on the river, Minister of Health Nou Beng told me that my wife and children and my sister had arrived home. I do not believe at all, because there have been two such cases already. The first time a woman about my age came to me and told me she was the wife of a foreign minister. She used me to call her husband to see him.
Excited to get out of the car, I immediately saw my wife Rany first, then my sister and a boy. My wife told me with tears in her eyes that this was our son. In fact, my wife and family hid to pick rice in Tbong Khmum district because the situation at that time was too chaotic and they did not dare to come forward.
My wife was informed that I was alive forever, but for her happiness she was willing to hide and endure hardships for a while. The reunion between me and my wife ended the widowhood situation where I was named a widower and Rany was named a widow with one child.
