The International Monetary Fund (IMF) Staff Visit team expects the banking sector to continue to play an important role in maintaining financial stability and contributing to macroeconomic stability and supporting growth in the post-crisis recovery phase.
The IMF Mission appreciated the measures taken by the Royal Government of Cambodia and the National Bank of Cambodia to respond quickly, timely and appropriately to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 in Cambodia and to support business activities to reduce yields. Socio-economic impact, especially on the poor.
The Royal Government has shifted its fiscal policy direction to the priority areas, and the National Bank of Cambodia has rapidly reduced its reserve requirements and issued guidelines to facilitate credit restructuring.
The IMF Mission is in line with the risk assessment and macroeconomic outlook of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and the National Bank of Cambodia.
For Cambodia, external demand is very uncertain, but the mission is optimistic about growth in key sectors of the Cambodian economy, especially manufacturing, while tourism will recover slowly.
Overall, the IMF recommends that the affected countries should: 1. Prioritize public health issues and measures to prevent the spread of Kovid-19;
2. Provide adequate support policies, both fiscal and monetary, and avoid removing these support policies too quickly.
3. Continue to maintain financial stability and monitor the risks of the financial system.
Reform structural issues with a focus on mitigating impacts and boosting potential for economic growth.
The IMF Mission also praised the effective and targeted use of technical assistance provided by the National Bank of Cambodia from the International Monetary Fund.
Therefore, the International Monetary Fund will continue to provide technical assistance as well as further capacity building in order to strengthen the resilience and development of the Cambodian banking sector.
Cambodia, Philippines discuss ways to reach double taxation agreement
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia and the Philippines on February 23, 2021 discussed ways to prepare a non-duplication agreement to increase trade and investment between the two countries. This is according to Mr. Koy Kuong, spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation.
"(Both sides) discussed the possibility of finding an agreement to avoid double taxation as soon as possible in order to increase investment and trade volume." This was confirmed by Mr. Koy Kuong to the media.
According to a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Cambodia-Philippines intends to discuss ways to increase the volume of trade or investment that has been small in the past. He said that in the past, the volume of trade and investment between the two countries in 2018 was more than 60 million US dollars, until 2019 it was nearly 100 million US dollars, but by 2020, due to Kovid 19, it will decrease slightly.
As for investment, Mr. Koy Kuong said that from 1994 to 2020, Philippine companies to invest in Cambodia is only about 10 million dollars.
