The official flag of the Swiss Confederation as we know it today dates back to 1840. There has been a lot of debate among historians about the choice of red. Some believe it is a symbol of the Blood of Christ, while others say the choice was inspired by the colors of the old Bernese flag. In 1848, it was officially used as the country's flag and was enshrined in the Swiss Charter.
Throughout its history, the Swiss flag has always had a unique feature that sets it apart from other countries. The Swiss flag is square, not rectangular.
