MANILA (Reuters) - The Philippine government's disaster agency on Sunday said it had evacuated more than 51,000 people to safety as tropical storm Dujuan approached, causing severe flooding. Submerged parts of the southern and central parts of the Philippines. This is according to the release by the Xinhua news agency on Sunday night, February 21, 2021.
Typhoons and tropical storms often hit the Philippines from June to December, killing hundreds and causing billions of dollars in damage. Located in the Pacific Ring of Fire, the Philippines is one of the countries most vulnerable to natural disasters, including volcanic eruptions, frequent earthquakes and typhoons, an average of 20 times a year.
