Malaria cases in Siem Reap by 2020 will decrease by 84% as health officials and people in the province actively participate in prevention
(Siem Reap): There were only 51 malaria cases in Siem Reap in 2020, a decrease of 84% compared to 304 cases in 2019, according to the report of the Siem Reap Provincial Health Department. Presented in a training course on malaria surveillance to health center officials at the Sokha Hotel Angkor in Siem Reap.
This training course was held for 3 days from 17-19 February 2021 under the chairmanship of Dr. Kros Sarath, Director of Siem Reap Provincial Health Department and the presence of trainers and 202 participants (79 women).
In Siem Reap, there are 35 health centers out of 93 that are considered to be malaria areas, of which 272 villages are in the malaria zone and there are 544 malaria treatment village volunteers who are continuing to work hard. The Ministry of Health is active and attentive in contributing to the complete eradication of malaria by 2025.
The report also points out that in order to achieve the National Policy on the Elimination of Malaria by 2025, many activities have been implemented, such as today the Siem Reap Provincial Health Department has opened a training course on malaria monitoring. Losing is a necessary task for the malaria eradication program, which requires the members of this training to pay close attention.
