Senior Minister Kun Kim Considers Partner Organizations as Important Contributors to Disaster Work
(Central): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice-Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, considers national and international civil society organizations to be an important partner in assisting in disaster management and problem solving in Cambodia.
In a meeting with representatives of development partners and dissemination of the National Action Plan for Disaster Risk Reduction 2019-2023 on February 18, 2021, Senior Minister Kun Kim noted that disasters caused by natural factors have become An acute issue that the Royal Government of Cambodia is paying close attention to.
The Senior Minister continued that based on this spirit, the Royal Government of Cambodia, headed by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has developed a National Action Plan for Disaster Risk Reduction 2019-2023.
The Senior Minister continued that the National Action Plan for Disaster Risk Reduction 2019-2023 shows the increase of responsibility at all levels by starting to address disaster risk related to climate change: drought, lack of rain. Insufficient rainfall, floods, flash floods, riverbanks, rivers, infectious diseases, epidemics, etc.
Senior Minister Kun Kim said that the National Action Plan for Disaster Risk Reduction 2019-2023 is a continuation of the vision of "building a safe and resilient community for sustainable national development."
The senior minister emphasized that the work of managing and resolving disasters caused by this requires close cooperation and cooperation between all relevant authorities. The Senior Minister also highlighted the values and indispensability of partner organizations.
According to Kun Kim, development partners have been heavily involved in disaster management and response in the past. On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia and on behalf of the National Committee for Disaster Management, Senior Minister Kun Kim expressed his deep gratitude to the development partners for their active contribution to the development of Cambodia in all areas, including disaster management. .
The Senior Minister also requested the partner organizations to contribute more to the work of disaster management in Cambodia and asked all partner organizations to use this national action plan to build a comprehensive program for the successful completion of their mission.
Both options could not be achieved or were chosen at the same time, and the administration of US President Joe Biden is now seen as trying to do just that. The United States has imposed new sanctions on Myanmar's military leadership, but it was not until 10 days after the military overthrew Aung San Suu Kyi's civilian government that Biden announced sanctions.
The crisis in Myanmar is also prompting the international community, especially the United States, to answer an important question: that is, when "democratic values" are in conflict with "geopolitical interests" as to which they should choose. Which one to take first? Sometimes diplomacy based on human rights is seen as acting as a court or a defender of justice, and sometimes it is a double standard and a hypocrisy.
However, this does not mean that the international community should ignore or recognize the military coup in Myanmar. The United States has the legal right to impose sanctions on the Myanmar military under a campaign against the rise of dictatorships around the world. Japan and the European Union may also join the Biden administration in this effort and to put more pressure on Myanmar's military regime.
