Ministry of Justice: New Royal Decree on pardons and commutation of sentences as a tool to encourage prisoners to change into good people (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Mr. Kim Santepheap, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Justice on 17 February The 2021 Decree states that the new Royal Decree on Pardon and Mitigation is an important tool for encouraging all prisoners to change for the better as soon as possible.
Mr. Kim Santepheap made this statement at a press conference on the outcome of the meeting on "Dissemination of the Royal Decree on Mitigation and Amnesty" and the strengthening of the amnesty work, which was chaired by Minister Keut Rith. Justice and Chairman of the National Commission to review and evaluate the list of prisoners requesting commutation and amnesty at the Ministry of Justice.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice stated: "This new Royal Decree is an Royal Decree that opens up opportunities for prisoners and encourages them to educate and change in order to receive the King's pardon. "Before there were three opportunities, now there are five opportunities, so this is a tool to encourage him to change in prison, which is a necessary condition for pardon."
On January 12, 2021, His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, issued a new Royal Decree on Mitigation and Pardon.
The same article further stipulates that commutation of sentences and pardons can also be done at major national festivals or events. In addition, mitigation and pardon can also be made for each of the above ceremonies, either separately or together.
Pursuant to Article 5 of the above Royal Decree, the mitigation and amnesty can be applied to prisoners who meet the following conditions:
* Awake, strive to change the concept of manners to become an honest citizen in order to integrate and participate in society. Actively and regularly reform education programs, vocational training programs and integration programs * Completely and accurately comply with other prison guidelines.
Article 7 of the new Royal Decree also stipulates that a convict can receive a commutation of his sentence only if he meets the conditions under Article 5 and receives one-third of the prison sentence set by the court. For life imprisonment, the convict must first be sentenced to 10 years.
In the same article, it is stated that the period of commutation of sentence shall be determined as follows:
Article 9 of the same Royal Decree also states that prisoners can receive a one-time commutation of their sentences within 12 months. In addition, a convict can receive a commutation of multiple sentences only if he or she has served half of the sentence imposed by the court, but must comply with the conditions set out in the first paragraph above.
In addition, convicts sentenced to life in prison after being reduced to 30 years may receive multiple commutations. However, even though the prisoner has been pardoned several times, the total sentence must be at least 20 years.
Pursuant to Article 10 of this Royal Decree, prisoners who meet the conditions under Article 5 can be pardoned if they are sentenced to imprisonment:
* 2/3 * A prisoner serving a life sentence can be pardoned if he has served at least 30 years or 20 years in the event of a reduced sentence.
Please be informed that before the issuance of this new Royal Decree, the request for commutation and amnesty for prisoners was made in only three major national festivals, including:
Mr. Kim Santepheap said that by 2020, a total of 1,753 prisoners, 113 of whom were women, had applied for pardons and commutations. Of these, 516 prisoners applied for pardon and 1,237 prisoners applied for commutation.
